Patch: compileFormat
Letter: K
Requires: none
Files: src/impl/formatter.js
Summary: Formatter: compile a format string to handlers once, and memoize the name each token resolves to

I: compile the format instead of interpreting it

The structural one, and the only patch here that is not a cache or a
short-circuit. Everything else leaves the shape of formatDateTimeFromString
alone: per formatted value it builds eight closures over `dt`, then walks the
token list calling a ~70-case string switch per token, and re-allocates the
Intl options object literals inside the branches it happens to take.

None of that depends on the value being formatted. Resolving each token to a
handler once per pattern turns formatting into a walk over a prebuilt array.
The representation is alternating literal/handler runs —
  lits[0] + fns[0]() + lits[1] + fns[1]() + ... + lits[n]
so literal text is concatenated rather than dispatched, and adjacent literals
collapse at compile time. That subsumes two of G's six: parseFormat now runs
once per pattern rather than once per value, and punctuation and unknown tokens
fold into the literal runs, which is what the interpreter's default branch
effectively did anyway.

The two locale-derived flags stay per call, passed as arguments rather than
baked into the program, so the cache can be keyed on the format string alone.

Implemented by delegating from the original method and leaving its body in
place as formatDateTimeFromStringInterpreted: it keeps the patch readable and
the two implementations diffable. A real PR would delete it.

The second half: the name a token resolves to

Compiling gets the dispatch out of the per-value path but leaves the most
expensive thing in it. A name token — month, weekday, era, day period — has an
English fast path that reads a constant array, and no fast path at all for
anything else: it calls loc.extract(), which builds an
Intl.DateTimeFormat.formatToParts() and searches its parts, once per token per
value. Formatting "cccc, LLLL d, yyyy 'at' h:mm a" is three of those per value
in fr and none in en, which is the whole of why the two are ~20x apart and why
the rest of this patch set — all of it aimed at zone lookups and the numeric
token path — never moved the non-English case.

What makes it a defect rather than a cost is that Locale has already computed
these. monthsCache, weekdaysCache and meridiemCache hold exactly these lists,
built once per Locale, and Info.months reads them while the formatter does not.
The same table shows both: Info.months in fr against a pattern naming a month
in fr, ~1ms per 20k against ~150ms.

The fix that suggests itself — have the formatter read Locale's lists — is
wrong, and quietly. Locale asks Intl for a format-context weekday with
year: "numeric" in the options and the formatter asks without it, which is
enough to change which form ICU returns: swapping them re-renders fi and fa
weekdays. So this memoizes the formatter's own extract() call instead, keyed by
the field the answer depends on — the month for a month, the weekday for a
weekday, the hour for a day period. Same options and same call as before, so
the string cannot change; just not recomputed for every value that shares a
field value with one already rendered.

Two things bound it, both found by sweeping locales rather than by reading:

  * Month and era names are only a function of dt.month and dt.year when the
    resolved calendar is Gregorian. "fa" resolves to the persian calendar with
    no outputCalendar named, and a slot keyed by the Gregorian month returns a
    neighbouring Persian one. Those two consult the resolved calendar first and
    fall back to extract() when it is not Gregorian, which keeps fa correct and
    still lets its weekday and day period memoize.
  * Day periods are keyed by hour across 24 slots rather than by hour < 12
    across two. No locale on the ICU this was written against renders more than
    AM/PM under h12, so the extra slots buy nothing measurable today; they are
    there because the opts name only the hour, which makes the hour the whole of
    what the answer can depend on, and keying on it directly assumes nothing.
    Two slots would import Locale#meridiems' assumption that a locale has
    exactly two day periods, which its own comment calls "probably wrong".

Verified byte-identical against the unpatched formatter over 39,600 renders:
33 locales, 24 patterns covering every name token at every width, timestamps
spanning all twelve months, all seven weekdays, all 24 hours, and a BC year.

One pre-existing inconsistency is left alone. toFormat("LLLL") in ja renders
"4" where Info.months and toLocaleString both render "4月": Locale#months
carries a ja workaround the formatter has never had. Memoizing does not change
it, since it memoizes what the formatter already returns.

